\begin{abstract}
We extended the surface element method proposed by Reitan and Higgins for calculating the capacitance of cubes, subdividing each face of a cube into up to $600 \times 600$ Subsquares. When each face was divided into $90 \times 90$ Subsquares, the capacitance of the unit cube reached a maximum value of $0.6608$ cm ($0.7352$ pF). We further applied this method to compute the capacitance of hollow cylinders by dividing them into $q$ annular rings (each $ 1$ cm in width), with each ring subdivided into $m$  square elements ($1$ cm side length). The capacitance of hollow cylinders under varying $q/m$ ratios was calculated and compared with Lekner’s numerical results and Cavendish’s experimental measurements, showing excellent agreement with both.  

\mykeywords{surface element method, capacitance, cube, hollow cylinder}
\end{abstract}
\maketitle


\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
The calculation of capacitance for a cube constitutes a classic challenge in electromagnetism, for which no analytical solution has been identified to date, and existing approaches rely exclusively on numerical methods. Polya~\cite{polya1947estimating} established the upper and lower bounds for the capacitance of a cube with edge length a : $0.62211a < C  < 0.71055a $ cm.  Reitan and Higgin~\cite{reitan1951calculation}  calculated its capacitance as  $ 0.6555a$ cm by dividing each face of the cube into $6 \times 6$  subsquares. Bai and Lonngren~\cite{bai2002capacitance} employed a similar approach, dividing each face of the cube into a maximum of $20\times20$  subsquares, and obtained a capacitance value of 0.6601a cm. Zhou and Szabo~\cite{zhou1994brownian} obtained a capacitance value of 0.6632a cm using the Brownian dynamics algorithm. Hwang~\cite{hwang2004electrical} obtained a capacitance value of 0.6606782a cm using the refined Brownian dynamics algorithm. Read~\cite{read1997improved} calculated a capacitance value of 0.6606785a cm using the modified Boundary Element Method. Brown~\cite{brown1990capacity} obtained a capacitance value of 0.661a cm using the finite difference method. 

The article is organized as follows. In Sec.~\ref{sec:cube}, we extend the Surface Element Method proposed by Reitan and Higgins by subdividing each face of a cube into both even and odd numbers of equal subdivisions, with the maximum subdivision reaching $600\times600$ subsquares per cube face. In Sec.~\ref{sec:cylinder}, we apply this enhanced surface element method to calculate the capacitance of hollow cylinders with arbitrary dimensions. Readers interested solely in the capacitance analysis of hollow cylinders may proceed directly to Sec.~\ref{sec:cylinder} without loss of continuity. The discussion and conclusions are presented in Sec.~\ref{sec:dis}. Details of this study are
listed in Apps.~\ref{appendix:ElectricPotential}-~\ref{appendix:Capacitance cube}.

\section{Capacitance of a Cube}
\label{sec:cube}
In the surface element method, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:Cube}, each face of the cube is divided into $7\times7$  subsquares (1 cm Side Length). Due to symmetry considerations, these $49$ subsquares are classified into ten distinct categories labeled by numbers $ 1, 2, 3 \cdots$10. Assuming that the charge carried by each subsquare is concentrated at its geometric center, the electric potential generated by a subsquare's own charge at its center can be determined through integration \eqref{eq:inter1}, yielding a value of $3.52549\sigma$. The potential contribution from other subsquares at this center is approximated as $\sigma/d$ \eqref{eq:inter2}, where d represents the distance between the centers of two subsquares and $\sigma$ denotes the charge density of the subsquare. By computing the cumulative electric potential at the center arising from all subsquares, we derive the following equation.
\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=7cm,height=7cm]{fig/cube.jpg}  
	\caption{Cube with Each Face Divided into $7\times7$ Subsquares  (1 cm Side Length).
 }
	\label{fig:Cube}
\end{figure}
\newpage
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
V_1 &= 9.052 \sigma_1 + 10.581 \sigma_2 + 8.743 \sigma_3 + 4.181 \sigma_4 + 4.760 \sigma_5 \\
&\quad + 8.685 \sigma_6 + 4.222 \sigma_7 + 4.232 \sigma_8 + 4.184 \sigma_9 + 1.040 \sigma_{10}, \\
V_2 &= 5.290 \sigma_1 + 13.204 \sigma_2 + 9.773 \sigma_3 + 4.553 \sigma_4 + 5.235 \sigma_5 \\
&\quad + 9.520 \sigma_6 + 4.597 \sigma_7 + 4.600 \sigma_8 + 4.539 \sigma_9 + 1.127 \sigma_{10}, \\
V_3 &= 4.371 \sigma_1 + 9.773 \sigma_2 + 13.117 \sigma_3 + 5.398 \sigma_4 + 4.959 \sigma_5 \\
&\quad + 10.215 \sigma_6 + 5.066 \sigma_7 + 4.881 \sigma_8 + 4.845 \sigma_9 + 1.199 \sigma_{10}, \\
V_4 &= 4.181 \sigma_1 + 9.106 \sigma_2 + 10.796 \sigma_3 + 8.515 \sigma_4 + 4.778 \sigma_5 \\
&\quad + 10.223 \sigma_6 + 5.473 \sigma_7 + 4.975 \sigma_8 + 4.981 \sigma_9 + 1.228 \sigma_{10}, \\
V_5 &= 4.760 \sigma_1 + 10.470 \sigma_2 + 9.919 \sigma_3 + 4.778 \sigma_4 + 7.851 \sigma_5 \\
&\quad + 10.748 \sigma_6 + 5.015 \sigma_7 + 5.155 \sigma_8 + 5.017 \sigma_9 + 1.244 \sigma_{10}, \\
V_6 &= 4.342 \sigma_1 + 9.520 \sigma_2 + 10.215 \sigma_3 + 5.111 \sigma_4 + 5.374 \sigma_5 \\
&\quad + 13.384 \sigma_6 + 5.637 \sigma_7 + 5.726 \sigma_8 + 5.549 \sigma_9 + 1.365 \sigma_{10}, \\
V_7 &= 4.222 \sigma_1 + 9.195 \sigma_2 + 10.132 \sigma_3 + 5.473 \sigma_4 + 5.015 \sigma_5 \\
&\quad + 11.274 \sigma_6 + 8.200 \sigma_7 + 5.744 \sigma_8 + 5.938 \sigma_9 + 1.428 \sigma_{10}, \\
V_8 &= 4.232 \sigma_1 + 9.199 \sigma_2 + 9.762 \sigma_3 + 4.975 \sigma_4 + 5.155 \sigma_5 \\
&\quad + 11.451 \sigma_6 + 5.744 \sigma_7 + 8.603 \sigma_8 + 6.635 \sigma_9 + 1.646 \sigma_{10}, \\
V_9 &= 4.184 \sigma_1 + 9.078 \sigma_2 + 9.690 \sigma_3 + 4.981 \sigma_4 + 5.017 \sigma_5 \\
&\quad + 11.098 \sigma_6 + 5.938 \sigma_7 + 6.635 \sigma_8 + 9.204 \sigma_9 + 1.945 \sigma_{10}, \\
V_{10} &=4.161 \sigma_1+ 9.015 \sigma_2+ 9.590 \sigma_3+ 4.914 \sigma_4 +4.977 \sigma_5,\\
&\quad + 10.922 \sigma_6+ 5.712 \sigma_7 + 6.582 \sigma_8+ 7.782 \sigma_9+ 4.476 \sigma_{10}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Based on the condition of electrostatic equilibrium, which requires the electric potentials to be equal, i.e., 
$V_1 = V_2 = V_3 = V_4 = V_5 = V_6 = V_7 =V_8 = V_9 = V_{10}= 1$ statvolt,
we solve to obtain:  
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\sigma_1 &= 0.02801750 ,   \quad 
\sigma_6 = 0.01131222 , \\
\sigma_2 &= 0.01978226 , \quad 
\sigma_7 = 0.01104003 , \\
\sigma_3 &= 0.01851476 , \quad 
\sigma_8 = 0.01038967 \\
\sigma_4 &= 0.01812616 , \quad
\sigma_9 = 0.01011398 , \\
\sigma_5 &= 0.01219925  , \quad
\sigma_{10} = 0.00983737 , \\
\end{aligned} 
\end{equation}
Therefore, the total charge $ Q $ can be derived.  
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
Q &= 24 \sigma_1 + 48 \sigma_2 + 48 \sigma_3 + 24 \sigma_4 + 48 \sigma_5\\ 
&+ 24 \sigma_6+ 24 \sigma_7 + 48 \sigma_8 + 24 \sigma_9+ 24 \sigma_{10}\\
&= 4.5975 \,\text{esu}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation} 
The capacitance $ C$ of a cube with edge length $a $ is  

\begin{equation}
    C=\frac{Q}{7V}a=0.6568a \, \text{cm}  = 0.7308a \,  \text{pF}
\end{equation}
We divided each face of the cube into $ 7\times7, 10\times10, 20\times20$, up to a maximum of $600\times600$ subsquares. The calculations showed that the capacitance reached a maximum value of $0.6608a \, \text{cm} $ when each face was divided into $90\times90 $ subsquares. As the number of divisions increased beyond this point, the calculated capacitance gradually decreased, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Cube capacitance} . Detailed capacitance values are provided in Table~\ref{tab:resultsB} of Appendix B.

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=8cm,height=8cm]{fig/cap.pdf}  
	\caption{Capacitance of a Unit Cube with Each Face Divided into $N\times N$ Subsquares (N: X-Axis).}
	\label{fig:Cube capacitance}
\end{figure}

The initial increase in capacitance can be explained using Thomson's theorem, which Maxwell first employed to estimate the capacitance of finite-length cylinder~\cite{cavendish1879electrical}. Thomson's theorem tells us that when a conductor reaches electrostatic equilibrium, its electrostatic field energy is minimized. The expressions for the electrostatic field energy $W$ and the total charge $Q$ can be written as follows
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
W &= \frac{1}{2}\int{V(\xi )\sigma (\xi )dS }\\  Q &=\int{\sigma (\xi )dS } 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Specifically, when the conductor reaches electrostatic equilibrium, its electrostatic field energy $W_0$ can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
{W_0} = \frac{1}{2}{V_0}Q = \frac{1}{2}\frac{Q}{C}Q = \frac{1}{2}\frac{{{Q^2}}}{C}
\end{equation}
Hence,
\begin{equation}
 C = \frac{{{Q^2}}}{{2{W_0}}}   
\end{equation}
According to Thomson's theorem, the electrostatic field
energy $W$ for any charge distribution satisfies $W \geq W_0$. Consequently, 
\begin{equation}
C = \frac{{{Q^2}}}{{2{W_0}}} \ge \frac{{{Q^2}}}{{2W}}\label{eq:t1}
\end{equation}

As the number of subareas increases, the charge distribution on the cube surface progressively approaches electrostatic equilibrium, leading to a gradual increase in capacitance.

The observed reduction in capacitance in the unit cube likely stems from the potential approximation in Formula \eqref{eq:inter2}. When two small squares are noncoplanar, the approximate formula \( q/d \) overestimates the potential value. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Cube}, the potential difference between the two labeled small squares (marked $10$) calculated using different formulas is  
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\frac{1}{\sqrt{3.5^2 + 3.5^2}}-\int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}}\int_{-\frac{1}{2}}^{\frac{1}{2}}\frac{2dxdy}{\sqrt{(3.5 - x)^2 + y^2 + 3.5^2}}\\
&=1.74526\times10^{-4}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

The fixed $1$ statvolt potential on each subarea leads to a lower charge density and reduced capacitance. As their number increases, this potential approximation becomes the dominant factor, causing the capacitance to decrease with more subareas. 


