\section{Electric Potential at the Center and in the Exterior Region of a Unit Square}
\label{appendix:ElectricPotential}
Assuming a square with a side length of $1$ as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:CubeA}, where the charge density $\sigma$ is uniformly distributed, the electric potential at the center of the square can be expressed as~\cite{dwight1988tables}
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
V &= 4\sigma \int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}} \int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}} \frac{dxdy}{(x^2 + y^2)^{\frac{1}{2}}}
   = 4\sigma \int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}} \sinh^{-1}\frac{1}{2y} dy\\
  &= 2\sigma \int_{1}^{\infty} u^{-2} \sinh^{-1}u du
   = 4\sigma \ln[1 + (2)^{\frac{1}{2}}]\\
   &= 3.52549\sigma\label{eq:inter1}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
