\section{Conclusion}

This work systematically challenges the common practice of relying on final-layer representations from pretrained molecular encoders. Our comprehensive study, spanning five diverse molecular encoders and 22 ADMET tasks, demonstrates that intermediate layers frequently offer superior downstream performance. This superiority is evident both with fixed embeddings, where in over 81\% of model-task combinations an intermediate layer outperformed the final-layer, yielding an average 5.4\% performance increase. During finetuning, intermediate layers led to notable improvements in over 71\% of cases, with an average gain of 8.5\%, alongside significant savings in parameters and compute time. These findings challenge the prevailing final-layer convention, highlighting substantial potential for predictive performance enhancement, while the strong positive correlation, median Pearson 0.60, supports an efficient \textit{evaluate-then-finetune} strategy.


% These findings challenge the prevailing final-layer convention  and highlight a significant potential for enhancing predictive performance. Furthermore, a strong positive correlation, median Pearson correlation of 0.60, between fixed embedding and finetuning performance per layer  supports a computationally efficient \textit{evaluate-then-finetune} strategy, offering more accurate and efficient models.

While our findings are encouraging, we acknowledge certain limitations. Although constrained by computational resources to five encoders and ADMET tasks, extending this analysis to a broader set of molecular encoders and chemical domains could further validate our findings. Future work with additional architectures, multiple random seeds, and more exhaustive hyperparameter searches would provide deeper insights and more robust generalizations.

In conclusion, our results strongly advocate for a shift beyond the final-layer default, urging exploration of the rich representational landscape within molecular encoders. By systematically evaluating intermediate layers, the field can unlock significant performance improvements and develop more efficient modeling pipelines in molecular science.








