\subsection{A Symplectic Example}


\begin{defn}
Let  $H:\mathbb{R}\times M\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ be a $C^{\infty}$-function and $(M,\omega)$ a symplectic manifold. The \textbf{Hamiltonian Vector Field} $X^{H}_t$ associated to $H_t:=H(t,-)$ is defined by 
\begin{equation*}\omega(X,X^{H}_t)=dH_t(X)\end{equation*}
 We define the \textbf{Hamiltonian isotopy} associated to $H_t$, denoted \begin{equation*}\phi^H_t:M\rightarrow M,t\in\mathbb{R}\end{equation*} 
 by 
    \begin{equation*}\frac{d\phi^H_t}{dt}=X^H_t\circ \phi^H_t.\end{equation*}

 Note each $\phi^H_t$ is a symplectomorphism, i.e., $(\phi^H_t)^*\omega=\omega$.




We can define the \textbf{group of Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms} of $(M,\omega)$ by,
\begin{equation*}\text{Ham}(M,\omega):=\{\varphi: M \to M | \varphi=\phi^H_1 \text{ for some Hamiltonian Isotopy }\phi_t^H\}\end{equation*}


\end{defn}
\begin{rmk}
Given some $\varphi\in \text{Ham}(M,\omega)$ there can be many Hamiltonians that generate it.
\end{rmk}

Now assume $M$ is compact (or just that any Hamiltonian is bounded), we then get for any given Hamiltonian $H:M \times I \to R$ a filtration on $M$, given by:
\begin{equation*}M^{\leq r}:= H_1^{-1}\{\leq r\}\end{equation*}
denote $(M,H_1)$ the corresponding filtered space. We can then view any $\varphi\in \text{Ham}(M,\omega)$ as a map (for some chosen Hamiltonian generating $\varphi$)
\begin{equation*}\varphi=\phi_1^H:(M_+,H_1) \to (M_+,H_1)\end{equation*}
where we have added a disjoint point to $M$ to be taken as the basepoint. We then set 
\begin{equation*}\lceil \varphi \rceil:= \sup_{x\in M} |H_1(\varphi(x))-H_1(x)|.\end{equation*}

If we then choose some Hamiltonian that generates $\varphi$ for all $\varphi\in \text{Ham}(M,\omega)$ we get a filtration on $\text{Ham}(M,\omega)$ itself, given by:

\begin{equation*}Ham^{\leq r}(M,\omega):=\{\varphi \in \text{Ham}(M,\omega): \lceil \varphi \rceil \leq r\}\end{equation*}


The issue is now, how to choose, for any given $\varphi\in \text{Ham}(M,\omega)$, a  Hamiltonian $H:I \times M \to \R$ that generates $\varphi$, in a coherent way. 

We could choose to set
\begin{equation*}\lceil \varphi \rceil=\in\ff_{H: \phi^H_1=\varphi}\{\sup_{x\in M}|H_1(\varphi(x))-H_1(x)|\}\end{equation*}

We therefore have 

\begin{equation*}\text{Ham}^{\leq r}(M,\omega):=\big\{\varphi \in \text{Ham}(M,\omega):\in\ff_{H: \phi^H_1=\varphi}\{\sup_{x\in M}|H_1(\varphi(x))-H_1(x)|\}\leq r \big\}\end{equation*}


Now consider 
\begin{equation*}Ham^{0}(M,\omega):=\bigcap_{\epsilon>0} \big(\text{Ham}^{\leq 0}(M,\Omega)\setminus \text{Ham}^{\leq -\epsilon}(M,\omega)\big) = \text{Ham}^{\leq 0}(M,\omega) \text{ if $|-|$ taken }\end{equation*}

this is the collection of all $\varphi \in \text{Ham}(M,\omega) $ such that 
\begin{equation*}|H_1(\varphi(x))-H_1(x)|=0\end{equation*}
for all $x\in M$ and (by ... for all choices of $H$), i.e., $\text{Ham}^0(M,\omega)$ is exactly the autonomous hamiltonian diffeomorphisms.
\begin{equation*}\text{Ham}^0(M,\omega)=\text{Aut}(M,\omega)\end{equation*}



\begin{rmk}
Note there is a natural basepoint, given by the identity morphisms (viewed as having zero shift). 
\end{rmk}

We wish to understand when the topology of $\text{Ham}^{\leq r}(M,\omega)$ changes, that is, for what values $\alpha \in \R$ do we have 

\begin{equation*}\pi_*(\text{Ham}^{\leq \alpha}(M,\omega)) \neq \pi_*(\text{Ham}^{\leq \alpha - \epsilon}(M,\omega))\end{equation*}
for all $\epsilon >0$.

In particular the topology of the connected component of the identity. 

Consider $\pi_1$, the existence of some $\alpha \in \R$ such that
\begin{equation*}\pi_1(\text{Ham}^{\leq \alpha}(M,\omega)) \neq \pi_1(\text{Ham}^{\leq 0}(M,\omega))\end{equation*}

implies either that we have attached some copies of $S^1$ to $\text{Aut}(M,\omega)$ or some copies of $D^2$, or a combination of the two.


\subsection{Surfaces $\S_g$}
Every $\varphi\in \text{Ham}(\S_g)$ can be written as the composition of finitely many autonomous hamiltonian diffeomorphisms.

\begin{equation*}\varphi= a_1 \circ ... \circ a_n \end{equation*}


want to construct maps

\begin{equation*}\circ_n : \text{Aut}(\S_g)^n \to Ham^{\leq r_n}(\S_g)\end{equation*}
for some $r_n\in \R$.

\subsubsection{A distance to Aut}

Consider $\varphi= a_n \circ \hdots \circ a_1$, for $a_i\in \text{Aut}(\S_g)$. Define 

\begin{equation*}d(\varphi,\text{Aut}(\S_g)):=\sup_{x \in \S_g}\in\ff_{H}\{\int_{\gamma(x,a_1, \hdots, a_n;\phi^H_1)}\omega\} \end{equation*}

where $\gamma(x,a_1,\hdots,a_n;\phi^H_1)$ is the loop formed by the points 
\begin{equation*}\{x,a_1(x),a_2 \circ a_1 (x), \hdots a_n \circ \hdots \circ a_1(x)\}\end{equation*}
and the paths given by correspoding flow lines for $a_i$, plus the flow line from $a_n \circ \hdots \circ a_1(x)$ to $x$ given by some hamiltonian diffeomorphism $\phi^H_1$. 

\begin{rmk}
If $\varphi \in \text{Aut}(\S_g)$ then 
\begin{equation*}d(\varphi,Aut(\S_g))=0\end{equation*}
\end{rmk}


















\section{Appendix and Comments}

When constructing $\ff \text{Top}_*$ one may wish to work with filtrations on a product space that come from an addition, i.e., if $f: X \to \R$ and $g: Y \to \R$ are action functionals defining filtrations on $X$ and $Y$ respectively, then the filtration of $X \times Y$ is induced by
\begin{equation*}f+g:X \times Y \to \R\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}(x,y) \mapsto f(x) + g(y)\end{equation*}

Explicitly, given two filtered spaces $(X,\ff_X)$ and $(Y,\ff_Y)$ the induced filtration on the product would be 

\begin{equation*}(X \times Y)^{\leq r}:= \bigcup_{s+t=r : s\geq\lfloor Y \rfloor, t\geq \lfloor X \rfloor}X(s)\times Y^{\leq t}\end{equation*}

However there is a difficulty in doing this, this comes firstly by realising that the diagonal map $\Delta_X$ is not well defined. A simple example to illustrate this is by taking both $X=Y$ to be the interval $[0,a]$ filtered by the filtration induced by the inclusion $i:[0,a] \hookrightarrow \R$, that is:

\begin{equation*}X(r):=\begin{cases}[0,a] & r\geq a\\
[0,r] & 0\leq r <a\\
* r<0\end{cases}\end{equation*}

now consider a map 

\begin{equation*}\Delta:X  \to X\times X\end{equation*}
we wish to be able to define this such that 
\begin{equation*}\Delta^{\leq r}:X ^{\leq r} \xrightarrow[]{}(X\times X)^{\leq r+\lceil \Delta \rceil}\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}x \mapsto (x,x)\end{equation*}
i.e., it acts as a diagonal map. However we find that 

\begin{equation*}\Delta^{\leq r}:X ^{\leq r} \xrightarrow[]{}\bigcup_{s+t=r+\lceil \Delta \rceil: s,t\geq 0}X^{\leq s }\times X^{\leq t}\end{equation*}
must have shift $\lceil \Delta \rceil\geq a$ in order for $X(r)$ to be included in $(X \times X)^{\leq r+\lceil \Delta \rceil}$ in the diagonal for all $r$. However since we can choose $a$ to be arbitrarily large, this shift is not well defined.

To fix this one could consider our morphisms to be multiplicative, i.e
\begin{equation*}f(r): X(r) \to Y^{\leq \alpha r}\end{equation*}
we can then view a diagonal map to be a morphsim of `expansion' equal to $2$, i.e., 

\begin{equation*}\Delta^{\leq r}X(r) \to (X \times X)^{\leq 2r}:\end{equation*}
this will again define a persistence category. However if we use the usual sphere with filtration concentrated at $1$, i.e., $(S^1,1)$, to suspend objects, then we find that (using the suitbale definition of smash product) suspending shifts filtrations additively, and hence is not well defined. Again we could fix this by considering suspending using the sphere $(S^1,0)$, however using this we cannot define homotopy groups that work well with the rest of the theory, in particlar we will not have that all filtered spectra can be represented by some filtered CW spectrum.  

One final option is to consider morphisms to come with both multiplicitve and additive term, i.e
 \begin{equation*}f(r): X(r) \to Y^{\leq ar+b}\end{equation*}

 this however breaks down quite quickly as affine maps are non commutative and hence shifting and `expanding' functors are not defined. Hence working with a category in which products are additive will not form a TPC, though may form some more complicated structure.

