For a face \( f \in F \), we denote by \( V(f) \) (resp. \( E(f) \)) the set of vertices (resp. edges) incident to \( f \). We write \( v < e \) (resp. \( v < f \)) to indicate that a vertex \( v \) is incident to an edge \( e \) (resp. a face \( f \)).  Furthermore, we say that \( v_1 \) and \( v_2 \) are \emph{connected by a face} if they are both contained in a common face, denoted \( v_1 \stackrel{F}{\sim} v_2 \).
We say that a subset \( W \subset V \) is \emph{connected} (resp. \emph{face-connected}) in \( \mathcal{D} \) if for any \( v, w \in W \), there exists a finite edge path (resp. face path)
\[
v = v_0 \sim \cdots \sim v_n = w \quad \text{(resp. } v = v_0 \stackrel{F}{\sim} \cdots \stackrel{F}{\sim} v_n = w\text{)}
\]
connecting \( v \) and \( w \). Given vertex sets \( W_1 , W \subseteq V \) with $W_1\subseteq W$, we say that \( W_1 \) is a \textbf{connected component} (resp. \textbf{face-connected component}) of \( W \) in \( \mathcal{D} \) if \( W_1 \) is connected (resp. face-connected) in \( \mathcal{D} \) and there is no other vertex set \( W_2 \subset W \), which is also connected (resp. face-connected), such that $W_1\subsetneq W_2$.

We call a vertex \( v \in V \) an \emph{interior vertex} if there exists a closed curve \( \gamma \subset \Omega \), which does not pass through \( v \), such that \( v \) is separated from all other vertices in \( V \setminus (\gamma \cup \{v\}) \) by \( \gamma \). Otherwise, we call \( v \) a \emph{boundary vertex}. We denote by \( \mathrm{Int}(V) \) and \( \partial V \) the sets of interior and boundary vertices, respectively.
 Given a subset \( W \subseteq V \), we define the \emph{outer boundary} of \( W \) as
\[
\tilde{\partial}(W) := \{ v \in V \setminus W : \exists w \in W \text{ such that } v \sim w \}.
\]
