The formation of the two-bounce window structure for $n=0$ is thus attributed to the resonant energy exchange mechanism. However, it is worth remembering that increasing the value of $n$ produces a potential with only one zero mode. As a result, the scattering process for $n\geq 1$ exhibits just bion-type behavior and still one-bounce for large values of $v$.

From now on, we present the results of the antikink-kink collision. The initial conditions for this case are as follows
\begin{eqnarray}
\phi(x,0,x_0,v) &=& \phi_{\bar{K}}( x+x_{0},0,v)  + \phi_{K}( x-x_0,0,-v)\text{,}\label{ic3} \nonumber
\\ 
\dot{\phi}(x,0,x_0,v) &=& \dot{\phi}_{\bar{K}}( x+ x_{0},0,v ) + \dot{\phi}_{K}(x-x_0,0,-v )\text{,} \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
where $\phi_{K}( x+ x_{0},t,v)$ means a boost of Lorentz for the static kink solution, with $\gamma=(1-v^2)^{-1/2}$ and $v$ representing the velocity. 

