For rasterization, we considered 3 m wide driveways. The size of the parking stalls was set to $6$ m $\times$ $3$ m, i.e., a parking field occupies two cells. At the polygon's boundaries, we can apply a threshold-based rule for including a cell in the parking lot depending on the fraction of its area contained within the polygon. For the experiments in this section, any cell that overlaps with the polygon is assumed to be part of the parking lot, and the other cells are marked as blocked cells. The final grid with R2 resolution is shown in Figure \ref{fig:resolution_r2}. 

All experiments were conducted on a Dell Precision workstation having a 32-core AMD Ryzen Threadripper Pro 7975WX CPU @ 4.0 GHz and 256 GB of RAM. The algorithms were coded in Python 3.10 with CPLEX 22.1.1, utilizing the callback functionality for the branch-and-cut algorithm and NetworkX for finding bidirectional hops and vertex and edge cut sets. A time limit of 900 s is set for each instance. Experiments were also conducted on a subset of instances with a time budget of two hours. Additional details supporting the main results, along with a comparison of the benefits of including the bidirectional hop inequalities in the branch-and-cut method, are provided in \ref{sec:add_stats}.

\subsection{Computational performance}
\label{sec:comp_performance}
\textbf{Analysis of one-way and two-way driving layout solutions:} Figure \ref{fig:upper_bounds} shows the best lower bounds of the three formulations---flow-based model discussed in Sections \ref{sec:MIP} and \ref{sec:one_way} (which is the baseline model), flow-based model with valid inequalities (bidirectional hop and dead end constraints), and a branch-and-cut version---for the one-way and two-way configurations. While many of these instances are solved optimally, others are terminated due to the time limit with an optimality gap. A total of 13291 stalls are discovered for the two-way version. An additional 2476 stalls can be accommodated if the drive layouts are one-way, which is an 18.63\% increase. The difference in the number of stalls between the one-way and two-way configurations is relatively less for smaller lots. However, it grows significantly with the parking lot area.  The absolute difference between the upper and lower bounds is shown in Figure \ref{fig:optimality_gap}. Out of 325 instances, the gap is zero for 245 two-way and 288 one-way instances. The absolute optimality gap in the two-way models was generally higher than in the one-way version when the time limits were reached. 

