\begin{abstract}
The rise of LLMs has driven demand for private serverless deployments, characterized by moderate-scale models and infrequent requests. 
While existing solutions follow exclusive GPU deployment, we take a step back to explore modern platforms and find that: 
Emerging CPU architectures with built-in accelerators are capable of serving LLMs but remain underutilized, and both CPUs and GPUs can accommodate multiple LLMs simultaneously.


We propose \oursys, a serverless inference scheme for small-to-mid-sized LLMs that enables elastic sharing across heterogeneous hardware.
\oursys tackles three fundamental challenges: 
(1) precise, fine-grained compute resource allocation at token-level to handle fluctuating computational demands; 
(2) a coordinated and forward-looking memory scaling mechanism to detect out-of-memory hazards and reduce operational overhead; 
and (3) a dual approach that reduces resource fragmentation through proactive preemption and reactive bin-packing.
Experimental results on 4 32-core CPUs and 4 A100 GPUs show that \oursys improves service capacity by 44\% - 63\% through sharing, while further leveraging CPUs boosts this to 91\% - 159\%.

\end{abstract}

