\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}
\textbf{Testbed.} 
The testbed consists of 4 32-core Intel Xeon 6462C @3.3 GHz CPU nodes and 4 NVIDIA A100-80GB GPU nodes. 

\begin{figure*}
	\centering
	\subfloat[\label{fig:e2e_3b}3B-sized cases.]
	{\includegraphics[width=.325\linewidth]{figures/e2e_3b.pdf}}
	\hspace{1mm}
	\subfloat[\label{fig:e2e_7b}7B-sized cases.]
	{\includegraphics[width=.325\linewidth]{figures/e2e_7b.pdf}}
	\hspace{1mm}
	\subfloat[\label{fig:e2e_13b}13B-sized cases.]
	{\includegraphics[width=.325\linewidth]{figures/e2e_13b.pdf}}
	\caption{Diverse performance metrics of each system under different model sizes and quantities.}
\end{figure*}


\textbf{Baselines.} 
We treat ServerlessLLM~\cite{DBLP:conf/osdi/FuXHBUPM24} as the first baseline, denoted as \texttt{sllm}, which only supports GPU nodes. 
Additionally, we have modified \texttt{sllm} to let it also utilize the CPU node. 
This is the second baseline, denoted as \texttt{sllm+cpu}. 


\textbf{Datasets and Workloads.} 
The input and output length of each request are sampled from the Azure LLM Conversation dataset~\cite{DBLP:conf/isca/PatelCZSGMB24}. 
Since there is no public multi-LLM workload, following ServerlessLLM, we use Azure Serverless Trace~\cite{DBLP:conf/usenix/ShahradFGCBCLTR20}, mapping each model to a function. 
We extracted the first 30-minute segment of the trace and uniformly sampled 32, 64, and 128 functions from it (illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:trace_timeline}). 

\textbf{Models.} 
We use popular LLMs with 16-bit precision of different sizes: Llama-3.2-3B, Llama-2-7B, and Llama-2-13B. 

\textbf{SLOs.} 
For a request of input length $L$, as discussed in \S \ref{sec:llm_inference_process}, we set TTFT and TPOT SLO to $\max(2, L/512)$ s and 0.25 s.

\textbf{Systems Behavior and Fairness.}
All systems prioritize the CPU nodes. 
All models are cached in CPU memory, and the cold-start procedure is similar across all systems, as \oursys utilizes \texttt{sllm}'s loader to enable fast loading. 
The keep-alive threshold is set to 1 s and all systems use the same inference engines: vLLM 0.5.2 and OpenVINO 2024.6.0. 


\texttt{sllm} triggers instance scale-out based on a default concurrency metric of 2, which leads to extreme inefficiency. 
Based on the profiling, we tried our best to conservatively tailor a set of higher concurrency values, which are (59, 15, 6) and (160, 32, 16) for 3B, 7B, and 13B models on CPU and GPU. 

\subsection{End-to-end Experiments}
\label{sec:eval_e2e}
In this section, we present diverse performance metrics of \oursys under different model sizes and quantities, comparing it with \texttt{sllm} and \texttt{sllm+cpu}. 
Figure \ref{fig:e2e_3b} shows the results for the 3B-sized cases, where 32, 64, and 128 replica models are generated from Llama-3.2-3B and mapped to the Azure Trace (Figure \ref{fig:trace_timeline}). 
Figure \ref{fig:e2e_7b} and \ref{fig:e2e_13b} depict the scenarios for the 7B-sized and the 13B-sized, respectively. 

\textbf{\oursys uses less resource with higher per-node throughput.} 
When serving 32 3B-sized models in Figure \ref{fig:e2e_3b}, \oursys consumes only 3.0 CPU nodes with 0 GPU, whereas \texttt{sllm} requires 3.2 GPU nodes. 
This is because \texttt{sllm} employs an exclusive approach, despite that GPUs offer high computational power. 
Using \texttt{sllm+cpu} can also reduce GPU usage by leveraging CPU resource, but it is less effective than \oursys. 
When serving 64 7B-sized models in Figure \ref{fig:e2e_7b}, \texttt{sllm+cpu} can only reduce the GPU usage from 3.9 to 3.5, whereas \oursys can achieve a reduction to 2.5. 

To further investigate the reasons behind \oursys's resource savings, we measured the average decode throughput per node. 
Compared to \texttt{sllm+cpu}, \oursys achieves throughput improvements by 6\%-52\% on CPU nodes and (-6)\%-86\% on GPU nodes. 
Note that the improvements can be negative since \oursys uses little GPU when serving 32 3B-sized models. 
The reasons for the improvements are twofold: 
First, \oursys can achieve and gain higher batch size. 
Second, \texttt{sllm+cpu} keeps the hardware compute resource idle during instance cold-start and recycle, while \oursys can schedule other instances instead.

Finally, as the number of models increases or model size grows, the resource usage gap between three systems gradually narrows. 
For example, when serving 128 13B-sized models (Figure \ref{fig:e2e_13b}), each system exhausts all nodes. 
This is because, on one hand, the excessive load begins to saturate each system, and on the other hand, larger models diminish the sharing potential of \oursys (detailed in \S \ref{sec:eval_mixed_deployment}).


\textbf{\oursys achieves superior service capacity with shorter TTFT tail latency.}
Under high loads, \oursys improves the number of SLO-compliant requests by 91\% - 159\% compared to \texttt{sllm} and by 44\% - 63\% compared to \texttt{sllm+cpu}. 
We also report the TTFT CDFs. 
Overall, \oursys does not experience worse tail latency due to resource sharing but does better. 
Unlike \oursys that uses shadow validation to verify the SLO, \texttt{sllm} and \texttt{sllm+cpu} experience long queuing under high load. 
Furthermore, when serving 128 models, the curves for \texttt{sllm} and \texttt{sllm+cpu} flatten at much lower percentiles, indicating that a significant portion of requests is dropped due to limited service capacity. 
Although \oursys also exhibits early flattening, it occurs much later.

\subsection{Ablation Study}
\begin{figure}
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{./figures/ablation_study.pdf}
	\vspace{-1mm}
	\caption{The resource usage and SLO compliance rate when disabling each component of \oursys.}
	\label{fig:ablation_study}
\end{figure}

We further study the effectiveness of \oursys's design. 
Figure \ref{fig:ablation_study} shows the results when disabling each component while serving 64 7B-sized models. 
Disabling any component results in a significant increase in GPU resource usage. Notably, after disabling sharing, the SLO compliance rate drops substantially to 89\%. 
This is because sharing is a key factor in increasing deployment density; without it, \oursys struggles to handle such a large number of models simultaneously. 

From the truncated timeline of GPU usage, we observe that after disabling the CPU, GPU usage is consistently high, whereas \oursys-full rarely exhausts all four GPU nodes. 
Additionally, after disabling defragmentation, when handling fluctuating loads (at 50 s and 250 s) and after load spikes, the GPU usage is notably higher compared to \oursys-full. 
This is because it creates fragmented instances to handle the surge, which cannot be reclaimed promptly. 


\subsection{Mixed Deployment}
\label{sec:eval_mixed_deployment}
Considering that real-world scenarios involve models of various sizes running simultaneously, we evaluate the effectiveness of \oursys in handling a mix of different model sizes in Figure \ref{fig:mixed_deployment}. 
When serving a combination of 3B, 7B, and 13B models, \oursys consistently requires fewer GPU nodes compared to \texttt{sllm+cpu}. 
However, its efficiency is more sensitive to model popularity trends. 
When small models dominate (4:1:1) compared to when large models dominate (1:1:4), there is a 1.2 GPU node usage difference, whereas sllm+cpu exhibits a smaller gap of only 0.7. 
This is because larger models impose higher computational and memory demands, limiting the efficiency of \oursys's sharing mechanism. 
When the model ratio is 1:1:4, both each node can only deploy around two model instances concurrently, whereas under a 4:1:1 ratio, it can support three to four instances. 
Since most popular models are relatively small~\cite{huggingface-popular}, \oursys can achieve significant reductions in resource usage in real-world deployments.

