\subsection{Methods for Comparison}
Our investigation based on the EconomicGrasp model, which we treat as a composition of five distinct modules: $m_1$ (\texttt{3D-UNet}), $m_2$ (\texttt{GraspableNet}), $m_3$ (\texttt{ViewNet}), $m_4$ (\texttt{CGGI-Net}), and $m_5$ (\texttt{GHLI-Net}). Our analysis in Section~\ref{sec:similarity_analysis} revealed that these modules exhibit heterogeneous convergence rates, with $m_1$ and $m_4$ converging faster than the others. This insight informs the design of our proposed methods and ablation studies. We compare the following approaches:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{isolated\_client} \textbf{(Baseline)}: A non-federated baseline where each client trains a model using only its own local data. There is no communication or aggregation with a server. This establishes a performance lower bound, representing the outcome without any collaboration.
    \item \texttt{t1} \& \texttt{t2} \textbf{(Baselines)}: Standard FedAvg where all model modules are trained in each communication round. We test with the number of local epochs set to $\tau = 1$ (\texttt{t1}) and $\tau = 2$ (\texttt{t2}) to evaluate the impact of additional local computation.
    \item FedLAMA \cite{lee2023layer} \textbf{(Baseline)}: An existing federated learning algorithm known for its layer-wise model aggregation strategy, included for a comprehensive comparison.
    \item \texttt{m2345} \& \texttt{m235} \textbf{(Ours)}: Our proposed module-wise FL method. These operate in a two-phase process per round: in Phase 1, all modules are trained for $\tau_1 = 1$ epoch. In Phase 2, a subset of modules $\tilde{M}$ undergoes an additional $\tau_2 = 1$ epoch of training. This strategy selectively excludes faster-converging modules. We test two variants:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \texttt{m2345}: Excludes only the backbone \texttt{m1}, setting $\tilde{M} = \{m_2, m_3, m_4, m_5\}$.
        \item \texttt{m235}: Excludes both \texttt{m1} and \texttt{m4}, setting $\tilde{M} = \{m_2, m_3, m_5\}$.
    \end{itemize}
    \item \texttt{m1} \& \texttt{m14} \textbf{(Ablation)}: To validate our design principle, these experiments invert our strategy by applying the Phase 2 update exclusively to the fastest-converging modules, $\tilde{M} = \{m_1\}$ and $\tilde{M} = \{m_1, m_4\}$, respectively.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Results and Analysis}
\textbf{Communication Efficiency.}
Figure~\ref{fig:seen_eval} illustrates the Average Precision (AP) on the \texttt{Seen} evaluation set as a function of total communication cost for each method. The plot clearly indicates that our proposed methods, \texttt{m235} and \texttt{m2345}, consistently outperform the baseline models (\texttt{t1}, \texttt{t2}, and \texttt{FedLAMA}) across all communication budgets. Notably, the two proposed methods exhibit distinct advantages at different stages of training. The \texttt{m2345} method achieves a high level of accuracy more rapidly in the initial training phase, suggesting that its strategy is beneficial for quick initial convergence. In contrast, while starting slightly slower, \texttt{m235} eventually surpasses all other methods to achieve the highest final performance. This indicates that its more focused strategy is effective for reaching peak accuracy. These findings validate that our module-wise strategy effectively utilizes a given communication budget to maximize model performance.

\textbf{Quantitative Performance.}
Table~\ref{tab:main_results} provides a detailed quantitative comparison of all methods. The \texttt{isolated\_client} baseline, representing training without any collaboration, serves as a performance lower bound. Although each client model converges quickly on its small local dataset (around 1000 seconds), it achieves a low \texttt{Overall} AP of only 31.49\%. This poor performance underscores the necessity of collaborative learning for developing robust and generalizable models. 

In contrast, all federated learning methods demonstrate significant improvements. At a fixed communication cost of 7.358 GB, our proposed method, \texttt{m235}, achieves the highest AP across all evaluation sets, including \texttt{Seen} (63.91\%), \texttt{Unseen} (49.82\%), and \texttt{Overall} (44.32\%), while our other variant, \texttt{m2345}, also shows strong performance by outperforming all baseline methods. Critically, \texttt{m235} achieves this superior performance with a lower computational cost (2588 sec.) compared to the stronger baselines \texttt{t2} (3268 sec.) and \texttt{FedLAMA} (2996 sec.). While the \texttt{t1} baseline is computationally cheaper (1642 sec.), its performance is significantly lower. This demonstrates that our methods offer a superior trade-off between performance and computational resources.

\textbf{Validation through Ablation Studies.}
To validate our core assertion that focusing additional training on slower-converging modules is beneficial, we conducted ablation studies (\texttt{m1} and \texttt{m14}). In these experiments, we inverted our strategy and applied the additional training phase exclusively to the fastest-converging modules (\texttt{m1} and \texttt{m4}, respectively). The results show that both \texttt{m1} and \texttt{m14} perform poorly, yielding significantly lower AP than our proposed methods and even the baselines. The performance of \texttt{m1} was the lowest among all tested configurations. This outcome strongly substantiates our module selection rationale, confirming that allocating additional resources to already-converged modules is an ineffective strategy.

In summary, the experimental results robustly demonstrate that our proposed module-wise FL strategies provide a more resource-efficient path to high-performance federated GPD, striking a superior balance between model accuracy, communication efficiency, and computational expenditure.

