\subsection{Real-world Experiments}
To further validate the practical applicability and performance of our proposed FL framework, we conducted grasping experiments on a physical robotic system. These experiments evaluated the model against inherent noise, calibration inaccuracies, and environmental variations that are not present in the simulation.

\textbf{Experimental Setup.} Our robotic setup consisted of a Universal Robots UR5e collaborative robot arm equipped with a Robotiq 2F-85 parallel gripper and an eye-in-hand Intel RealSense D435 depth camera for point cloud capture. A host PC running ROS2 served as the control station for communication and grasp execution. To create challenging grasping scenarios, we used a variety of common household and workshop objects, placing 6 to 8 of them in a cluttered arrangement for each experimental run. The complete setup and object set are depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:robot_setup}.

\textbf{Methods and Evaluation.} We compared the performance of two representative models: \texttt{t1}, serving as the standard FedAvg baseline, and \texttt{m235}, representing our proposed module-wise FL framework. The evaluation task for both models was to clear a cluttered scene of all its objects. For each run, we measured the total number of grasp attempts required to complete this task. A grasp was deemed successful if an object was securely lifted from the surface. The Success Rate (SR) for each run was then calculated as the ratio of successful grasps to the total number of attempts.

\textbf{Results and Discussion.} The results of our real-world experiments are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:real_world_grasping}. The baseline \texttt{t1} model achieved an overall success rate (SR) of 62\% across 56 total attempts. In contrast, our proposed method, \texttt{m235}, demonstrated superior performance, attaining a higher overall SR of 69\%. Notably, \texttt{m235} was also more efficient, requiring fewer total attempts to clear all the objects in the scenes. This performance advantage indicates that the model trained with our module-wise strategy generates more reliable and effective grasp poses in a real-world environment. These experiments empirically validate that our proposed FL framework outperforms standard FedAvg not only in simulation but also in practical, physical applications, yielding a model with enhanced robustness and efficiency.

\section{Conclusion}
To address the high communication cost of federated learning on resource-constrained robots, we proposed a module-wise framework that reduces data transmission by selectively updating model components. We validated our framework on grasp pose detection, where it demonstrated improved efficiency and performance. Since modular architectures are common in models for other robotics tasks, our approach has the potential to be applied more broadly as an efficient training strategy for other decentralized robotic systems.

