\section{Introduction}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The quantized six-vertex model (q-6v model, for short) with four
parameters was introduced by Kuniba, Matsuike and Yoneyama in
\cite{KMY23}, generalizing \cite{BS06, BMS10}. The model is given by
an operator $\mathscr{L}=\mathscr{L}(r,s,f,g;q)$ in
$\mathrm{End}(V \otimes V) \otimes \mathcal{W}(q)$, where $V$ is a
two-dimensional vector space, $\mathcal{W}(q)$ is the $q$-Weyl
algebra, and $r,s,f,g \in \mathbb{C}$ are parameters. Its graphical
representation is Figure \ref{fig:6v}, where one sees the weight
conservation, $i+j = a+b$, in $V \otimes V$ as in the (original)
six-vertex model \cite{B82}.

